  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186175973,
      "id": "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb",
      "title": "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,3\\sec t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{9}-\\frac{y^2}{4}=1,\\qquad z\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $z$ and $y$; $x$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{4}=\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-1=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nChoose $y=2\\tan t$, which takes every real value on the given interval. This is the positive-tangent orientation used in the choices. The scale is $2=\\sqrt{4}$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=2\\tan t,\\qquad \\mathbf{f}(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,2\\tan t,\\,3\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-\\frac{(2\\tan t)^2}{4}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $x$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the second component is $2\\tan t$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "91d852a8-9701-5d81-a0cb-4f2059db0db1",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
