  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186175973,
      "id": "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb",
      "title": "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,3\\tan t,\\,z\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}-\\frac{y^2}{9}=1,\\qquad x\\ge 4.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $x$ and $y$; $z$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}=1+\\frac{(3\\tan t)^2}{9}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ge 4$ and $\\sec t\\ge1$ on the given interval, take the positive root. The scale is $4=\\sqrt{16}$. Thus\n\n$$\nx=4\\sec t,\\qquad \\mathbf{f}(t,z)=\\langle 4\\sec t,\\,3\\tan t,\\,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(4\\sec t)^2}{16}-\\frac{(3\\tan t)^2}{9}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $z$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the first component is $4\\sec t$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "dc853c27-5480-5759-8aa2-a83d4d32d8d2",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
