    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,3\\tan t,\\,\\text{_____}\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{1}-\\frac{y^2}{9}=1,\\qquad z\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $z$ and $y$; $x$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{1}=1+\\frac{(3\\tan t)^2}{9}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ge 1$ and $\\sec t\\ge1$ on the given interval, take the positive root. The scale is $1=\\sqrt{1}$. Thus\n\n$$\nz=\\sec t,\\qquad \\mathbf{f}(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,3\\tan t,\\,\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\sec t)^2}{1}-\\frac{(3\\tan t)^2}{9}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $x$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the third component is $\\sec t$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "782b9c9b-c98e-5341-b343-7b218433d257",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
